1
00:00:04,900 --> 00:00:18,475
This is our planet, exactly as it looked on the twenty third of September, twenty twenty six, the day of the equinox, photographed every thirty minutes by a weather satellite parked thirty six thousand kilometers above the equator.

2
00:00:20,858 --> 00:00:21,539
Clouds.

3
00:00:21,959 --> 00:00:22,689
Storms.

4
00:00:23,109 --> 00:00:25,783
A great swirling storm off the coast of Mexico.

5
00:00:26,203 --> 00:00:28,376
Daylight sweeping across two continents.

6
00:00:28,796 --> 00:00:30,659
Everything you can see here is real.

7
00:00:31,079 --> 00:00:35,670
But the most important thing happening in this picture is something you cannot see at all.

8
00:00:38,067 --> 00:00:40,847
Wrapped around this world is a thin layer of gas.

9
00:00:41,267 --> 00:00:48,514
Mixed into it, at a concentration of roughly four hundred and thirty molecules in every million, is carbon dioxide.

10
00:00:48,934 --> 00:00:50,634
It has no color and no smell.

11
00:00:51,054 --> 00:00:54,617
It is the breath of every animal and the food of every plant.

12
00:00:55,037 --> 00:01:03,328
And over the last century and a half, we have changed its amount faster than at any time in the geological record that ice cores can read.

13
00:01:05,733 --> 00:01:07,599
Here is a number to keep in mind.

14
00:01:08,019 --> 00:01:14,494
Human activity now releases about forty three billion tonnes of carbon dioxide every year.

15
00:01:14,914 --> 00:01:17,875
That is close to fourteen hundred tonnes every second.

16
00:01:18,295 --> 00:01:21,283
In the corner of your screen, a counter has just started.

17
00:01:21,703 --> 00:01:26,644
It shows how much carbon dioxide humanity releases while you watch this film.

18
00:01:29,025 --> 00:01:30,204
Where does all of it go?

19
00:01:30,704 --> 00:01:31,747
Who releases it?

20
00:01:32,247 --> 00:01:36,010
Which forests and oceans are absorbing it, and which ones are giving up?

21
00:01:36,510 --> 00:01:42,985
For most of history, these were questions nobody could answer, because nobody could see the whole planet at once.

22
00:01:43,405 --> 00:01:45,577
So NASA built a machine to weigh the air.

23
00:01:45,997 --> 00:01:46,902
From space.

24
00:01:59,817 --> 00:02:01,761
The story begins on a volcano.

25
00:02:02,181 --> 00:02:13,385
In March of nineteen fifty eight, a young chemist named Charles David Keeling began measuring carbon dioxide at a small station high on the slopes of Mauna Loa, in Hawaii.

26
00:02:13,805 --> 00:02:24,269
He chose it because the air there, more than three thousand meters up and thousands of kilometers from any continent, is about as clean and well mixed as air on Earth gets.

27
00:02:26,650 --> 00:02:32,250
His very first monthly average was three hundred and fifteen point seven parts per million.

28
00:02:32,670 --> 00:02:35,122
Within a couple of years, he noticed two things.

29
00:02:35,542 --> 00:02:36,901
The first was a rhythm.

30
00:02:37,321 --> 00:02:41,682
Every spring the level fell, and every autumn it rose again, like breathing.

31
00:02:44,067 --> 00:02:46,901
The second thing was quieter, and far more important.

32
00:02:47,321 --> 00:02:51,570
Underneath the breathing, each year's average sat a little higher than the last.

33
00:02:51,990 --> 00:02:53,321
Keeling kept measuring.

34
00:02:53,741 --> 00:02:55,535
His son Ralph took over the record.

35
00:02:55,955 --> 00:02:58,153
NOAA built its own instruments beside it.

36
00:02:58,573 --> 00:03:00,155
The measurements have never stopped.

37
00:03:02,567 --> 00:03:05,450
This is what nearly seventy years of patience looks like.

38
00:03:05,870 --> 00:03:10,682
Every point on this line is a monthly average of real measurements from Mauna Loa.

39
00:03:11,102 --> 00:03:16,149
It is called the Keeling Curve, and it may be the most important graph of the modern age.

40
00:03:18,567 --> 00:03:20,094
And it is not just Hawaii.

41
00:03:20,514 --> 00:03:26,535
NOAA's global network of sampling sites, from the Arctic to the South Pole, tells the same story.

42
00:03:26,955 --> 00:03:32,199
The thinner line here is the global average at the surface, starting in nineteen seventy nine.

43
00:03:32,619 --> 00:03:39,533
It tracks Mauna Loa almost perfectly, with a smaller seasonal swing because it blends both hemispheres together.

44
00:03:41,942 --> 00:03:50,695
In May of twenty twenty six, the monthly average reached four hundred and thirty two point three parts per million, the highest ever recorded there.

45
00:03:51,115 --> 00:03:58,886
That is thirty seven percent more than when Keeling started, and more than fifty percent above the level before the industrial revolution.

46
00:04:01,275 --> 00:04:13,343
For context, air bubbles trapped in Antarctic ice tell us carbon dioxide stayed between roughly one hundred and eighty and three hundred parts per million for at least eight hundred thousand years.

47
00:04:13,763 --> 00:04:18,199
It rose and fell with the ice ages, slowly, over thousands of years.

48
00:04:18,619 --> 00:04:22,702
We have pushed it far outside that range in about a century and a half.

49
00:04:25,108 --> 00:04:27,122
Parts per million can sound tiny.

50
00:04:27,542 --> 00:04:29,024
Picture a million marbles.

51
00:04:29,444 --> 00:04:32,687
Four hundred and thirty of them are carbon dioxide.

52
00:04:33,107 --> 00:04:36,126
Before the industrial age it was two hundred and eighty.

53
00:04:36,546 --> 00:04:48,806
That small handful of extra marbles, spread through the entire atmosphere, adds up to hundreds of billions of tonnes of extra carbon, and it is enough to change how much heat the planet holds onto.

54
00:04:51,192 --> 00:04:54,199
But there is a problem with a curve measured on one mountain.

55
00:04:54,619 --> 00:04:56,416
It tells you the total is rising.

56
00:04:56,836 --> 00:05:00,626
It cannot tell you where the carbon is coming from, or where it is going.

57
00:05:01,046 --> 00:05:04,895
Ground stations grew into a global network, but they are sparse.

58
00:05:05,315 --> 00:05:08,782
Most sit in North America, Europe and on remote islands.

59
00:05:09,202 --> 00:05:17,283
Over the Amazon, Central Africa, Siberia and the open ocean, there were vast regions where almost nobody was measuring.

60
00:05:17,703 --> 00:05:20,518
To see the whole planet breathe, you would have to leave it.

61
00:05:26,400 --> 00:05:28,490
The first attempt ended within minutes.

62
00:05:28,910 --> 00:05:39,166
On the twenty fourth of February, two thousand nine, NASA's original Orbiting Carbon Observatory lifted off from Vandenberg in California on a Taurus rocket.

63
00:05:39,587 --> 00:05:44,299
The protective nose cone, the fairing, was supposed to split open and fall away.

64
00:05:44,719 --> 00:05:45,989
It never separated.

65
00:05:46,409 --> 00:05:53,827
Weighed down by the extra mass, the rocket could not reach orbit, and the observatory fell into the ocean near Antarctica.

66
00:05:56,233 --> 00:05:57,952
Years of work were gone in minutes.

67
00:05:58,372 --> 00:06:02,164
But the science case had not changed, and the team had not given up.

68
00:06:02,584 --> 00:06:09,094
NASA approved a near copy, built largely to the same design, with lessons from the loss folded in.

69
00:06:09,514 --> 00:06:11,095
They called it OCO-2.

70
00:06:13,483 --> 00:06:21,846
At two fifty six in the morning, on the second of July, twenty fourteen, a Delta II rocket climbed out of Vandenberg into the dark.

71
00:06:22,266 --> 00:06:23,956
This time, the fairing opened.

72
00:06:24,376 --> 00:06:30,299
Less than an hour later, OCO-2 separated from the rocket and unfolded its solar panels.

73
00:06:30,719 --> 00:06:32,148
The second chance had worked.

74
00:06:34,567 --> 00:06:44,377
OCO-2 flies seven hundred and five kilometers up, in a polar orbit that carries it almost from pole to pole, once every ninety nine minutes.

75
00:06:44,797 --> 00:06:49,021
As Earth turns beneath it, each pass crosses a new strip of the planet.

76
00:06:51,442 --> 00:06:52,976
Watch how the tracks build up.

77
00:06:53,396 --> 00:06:55,453
About fourteen and a half orbits a day.

78
00:06:55,873 --> 00:07:01,020
After sixteen days, two hundred and thirty three orbits, the pattern repeats exactly.

79
00:07:01,440 --> 00:07:14,335
This is a sun synchronous orbit: the satellite always crosses the equator at about one thirty six in the afternoon local time, when the Sun is high and the light reflected from the ground is strongest.

80
00:07:16,733 --> 00:07:36,722
For most of its life, OCO-2 has flown at the head of a formation that scientists nicknamed the A Train: a line of Earth observing satellites crossing the same ground minutes apart, so that measurements of clouds, aerosols, water vapor and carbon can be stitched together into one picture of the same moment.

81
00:07:42,608 --> 00:07:49,489
So how do you measure a gas you cannot see, through the whole depth of the atmosphere, from seven hundred kilometers away?

82
00:07:49,989 --> 00:07:51,023
You use sunlight.

83
00:07:51,443 --> 00:07:57,978
Sunlight passes down through the air, reflects off the ground, and travels back up through the air to the satellite.

84
00:07:58,398 --> 00:07:59,776
Twice through the atmosphere.

85
00:08:00,196 --> 00:08:05,144
Along the way, every carbon dioxide molecule it meets can absorb a little of it.

86
00:08:07,525 --> 00:08:08,960
But molecules are picky.

87
00:08:09,380 --> 00:08:17,114
Carbon dioxide only absorbs light at very specific wavelengths, set by the way its atoms vibrate and rotate.

88
00:08:17,534 --> 00:08:24,947
Spread sunlight out finely enough and those wavelengths show up as dark lines, a pattern as distinctive as a barcode.

89
00:08:25,367 --> 00:08:29,750
The more carbon dioxide the light passed through, the deeper those lines become.

90
00:08:32,150 --> 00:08:36,328
OCO-2 carries three spectrometers that share one telescope.

91
00:08:36,748 --> 00:08:40,942
One looks at carbon dioxide near one point six one microns.

92
00:08:41,362 --> 00:08:46,727
One looks at a stronger carbon dioxide band near two point zero six microns.

93
00:08:47,147 --> 00:08:51,467
And the third looks at oxygen, near zero point seven six microns.

94
00:08:51,887 --> 00:08:55,512
Each splits the light into more than a thousand separate colors.

95
00:08:57,900 --> 00:08:58,875
Why oxygen?

96
00:08:59,375 --> 00:09:01,131
Because oxygen is the ruler.

97
00:09:01,551 --> 00:09:06,320
It makes up a steady twenty point nine five percent of dry air everywhere on Earth.

98
00:09:06,740 --> 00:09:14,397
So the oxygen lines reveal exactly how much air the light passed through, mountains or sea level, clear sky or haze.

99
00:09:14,817 --> 00:09:30,008
Divide the carbon dioxide signal by the amount of air, and you get the quantity scientists call XCO₂: the average concentration of carbon dioxide in the entire column of air, from the ground to the edge of space.

100
00:09:32,400 --> 00:09:34,656
And it has to be extraordinarily precise.

101
00:09:35,076 --> 00:09:41,640
Carbon sources and sinks change that column by only a few parts per million out of more than four hundred.

102
00:09:42,060 --> 00:09:48,830
To be useful, each measurement needs a precision of about one part per million, roughly a quarter of one percent.

103
00:09:49,250 --> 00:09:55,780
That is like weighing a full suitcase so accurately that you notice someone slipped in a single extra sock.

104
00:09:58,192 --> 00:10:00,911
The instrument records three frames every second.

105
00:10:01,331 --> 00:10:13,410
Each frame captures eight side by side footprints, each about one point three kilometers wide and two and a quarter kilometers long, together forming a swath roughly ten kilometers wide.

106
00:10:13,830 --> 00:10:17,1000
Twenty four measurements per second, on the sunlit side of every orbit.

107
00:10:20,400 --> 00:10:23,341
That adds up to roughly a million measurements a day.

108
00:10:23,761 --> 00:10:30,227
But many are spoiled by clouds or thick haze, because the light has to reach the ground and come back cleanly.

109
00:10:30,647 --> 00:10:39,202
After strict screening, a much smaller set, typically on the order of a hundred thousand good soundings a day, goes into the best quality data.

110
00:10:39,622 --> 00:10:44,452
Even so, that is more than every ground station on Earth combined could ever collect.

111
00:10:46,858 --> 00:10:50,586
Turning those spectra into a single number is a feat of physics.

112
00:10:51,006 --> 00:11:02,824
For every sounding, a computer model simulates how sunlight would travel through the atmosphere, accounting for surface pressure, temperature, water vapor, clouds and tiny airborne particles.

113
00:11:03,244 --> 00:11:08,949
It adjusts all of them until the simulated spectrum matches the measured one line for line.

114
00:11:09,369 --> 00:11:11,722
What remains is the carbon dioxide.

115
00:11:12,142 --> 00:11:18,912
Then the results are checked, and corrected for small biases, against independent measurements made from the ground.

116
00:11:21,317 --> 00:11:25,588
Over land, OCO-2 can look straight down at the ground beneath it.

117
00:11:26,008 --> 00:11:32,288
Over the dark ocean it tilts toward the bright spot where sunlight glints off the water, to catch enough light.

118
00:11:32,708 --> 00:11:45,871
And regularly it stares at one place while flying past, locking onto ground stations of the Total Carbon Column Observing Network, so that every satellite measurement can be checked against instruments on the surface.

119
00:11:48,275 --> 00:11:51,418
Then came a bonus nobody had planned the mission around.

120
00:11:51,838 --> 00:12:01,795
When plants photosynthesize, their chlorophyll re-emits a tiny fraction of the absorbed sunlight as a faint red glow, called solar induced fluorescence.

121
00:12:02,215 --> 00:12:12,576
It is far too weak to see with your eyes, but inside the dark Fraunhofer lines of the solar spectrum, where sunlight is nearly absent, OCO-2 can detect it.

122
00:12:12,996 --> 00:12:19,765
For the first time, scientists could watch photosynthesis itself, the planet actually inhaling, from orbit.

123
00:12:25,650 --> 00:12:27,105
Remember Keeling's rhythm?

124
00:12:27,605 --> 00:12:29,157
Here it is, isolated.

125
00:12:29,577 --> 00:12:38,880
Take away the long term rise, and the Mauna Loa record shows the same yearly breath, peaking in May and bottoming out around September or October.

126
00:12:39,300 --> 00:12:40,959
Each line is a different era.

127
00:12:43,358 --> 00:12:45,090
The reason is geography.

128
00:12:45,510 --> 00:12:49,914
Most of Earth's land, and most of its forests, lie in the Northern Hemisphere.

129
00:12:50,334 --> 00:12:58,828
Every northern spring, billions of trees leaf out across North America, Europe and Siberia and pull carbon dioxide out of the air.

130
00:12:59,248 --> 00:13:03,125
Every autumn, the leaves fall and decay, and the carbon comes back.

131
00:13:03,545 --> 00:13:07,151
The whole hemisphere inhales in summer and exhales in winter.

132
00:13:09,567 --> 00:13:11,304
And the breath itself is changing.

133
00:13:11,724 --> 00:13:22,712
At Mauna Loa, the swing between the spring peak and the autumn low has grown over the decades, from under six parts per million in the nineteen sixties to more than six and a half today.

134
00:13:23,132 --> 00:13:29,665
Far to the north, in Alaska and the Arctic, the yearly swing is much larger still, and has grown faster.

135
00:13:30,085 --> 00:13:35,155
Northern ecosystems are breathing more deeply in a warmer, carbon rich world.

136
00:13:37,567 --> 00:13:42,000
In the Southern Hemisphere, which is mostly ocean, the breathing is much fainter.

137
00:13:42,420 --> 00:13:46,788
At the South Pole, the yearly swing is only around a single part per million.

138
00:13:47,208 --> 00:13:54,464
The planet's breath is lopsided, and the satellite sees that asymmetry every single day, sweeping from pole to pole.

139
00:13:56,858 --> 00:14:00,585
Now look at how fast the total has been rising, year by year.

140
00:14:01,005 --> 00:14:07,830
In the early nineteen sixties, Mauna Loa's annual average rose by less than one part per million each year.

141
00:14:08,250 --> 00:14:11,011
In the twenty tens, it was close to two and a half.

142
00:14:11,431 --> 00:14:17,048
In twenty twenty four, it jumped by three and a half: the largest annual rise in the record.

143
00:14:19,442 --> 00:14:21,387
Notice that the bars are not smooth.

144
00:14:21,807 --> 00:14:27,046
Some years leap upward, like nineteen ninety eight, twenty sixteen and twenty twenty four.

145
00:14:27,466 --> 00:14:30,457
Human emissions do not swing that much from year to year.

146
00:14:30,877 --> 00:14:35,037
Something in nature is changing how much carbon the planet absorbs.

147
00:14:35,457 --> 00:14:36,818
That something has a name.

148
00:14:37,238 --> 00:14:42,064
And it was OCO-2 that showed us, continent by continent, how it works.

149
00:14:47,983 --> 00:14:54,036
In twenty fifteen, a giant pool of unusually warm water spread across the tropical Pacific.

150
00:14:54,456 --> 00:15:01,865
This is real data from NOAA: satellite and buoy measurements of sea surface temperature, shown as the difference from normal.

151
00:15:02,285 --> 00:15:03,854
Red is warmer than usual.

152
00:15:04,274 --> 00:15:06,104
Watch the equatorial Pacific.

153
00:15:08,483 --> 00:15:14,685
This is El Niño, and the one of twenty fifteen and twenty sixteen was among the strongest ever measured.

154
00:15:15,105 --> 00:15:20,593
It reshuffles weather around the world, bringing drought and heat to some regions and floods to others.

155
00:15:21,013 --> 00:15:26,451
And it arrived just as OCO-2 had begun sending back its first full year of data.

156
00:15:28,858 --> 00:15:34,293
In twenty seventeen, the mission's scientists published a set of studies in the journal Science.

157
00:15:34,713 --> 00:15:48,131
By comparing twenty fifteen with the calmer year of twenty eleven, they found that the tropical land regions of the world released about two and a half billion tonnes more carbon into the atmosphere during the El Niño year.

158
00:15:48,551 --> 00:15:54,460
That extra pulse helped push the global growth rate of carbon dioxide to a record at the time.

159
00:15:56,858 --> 00:16:01,512
The surprise was that the three tropical continents did it in three different ways.

160
00:16:01,932 --> 00:16:08,612
In South America, severe drought meant the rainforest photosynthesized less, so it absorbed less carbon.

161
00:16:09,032 --> 00:16:16,066
In Africa, rainfall was near normal but higher temperatures sped up decay, so soils and plants breathed out more.

162
00:16:16,486 --> 00:16:23,108
And in Indonesia, drought dried out carbon rich peatlands, and fires released enormous amounts of carbon.

163
00:16:23,528 --> 00:16:24,585
Same El Niño.

164
00:16:25,005 --> 00:16:26,449
Three different responses.

165
00:16:28,858 --> 00:16:33,474
This is exactly the kind of insight a single mountaintop could never provide.

166
00:16:33,894 --> 00:16:36,805
The curve at Mauna Loa told us that something happened.

167
00:16:37,225 --> 00:16:39,955
The satellite told us where, and suggested why.

168
00:16:40,375 --> 00:16:51,210
And it carries a warning: in a hotter, drier future, the tropical forests that have long absorbed part of our emissions could absorb less, or even turn into sources.

169
00:16:57,108 --> 00:17:06,062
Carbon dioxide from a power plant or a city is quickly diluted into the global background, which makes it fiendishly hard to spot from space.

170
00:17:06,482 --> 00:17:10,410
But burning fossil fuel releases other gases at the same time.

171
00:17:10,830 --> 00:17:21,738
This is nitrogen dioxide, measured by the European TROPOMI instrument on the Sentinel-5P satellite over the winter of twenty twenty four to twenty twenty five.

172
00:17:22,158 --> 00:17:26,528
Every bright patch is combustion: traffic, industry, power stations.

173
00:17:28,942 --> 00:17:32,306
Zoom in, and the map becomes a portrait of human activity.

174
00:17:32,726 --> 00:17:34,455
The industrial heart of Europe.

175
00:17:34,875 --> 00:17:36,409
The Indo-Gangetic plain.

176
00:17:36,829 --> 00:17:37,802
Eastern China.

177
00:17:38,222 --> 00:17:45,641
Nitrogen dioxide lasts only hours, so it stays close to where it was emitted, a fingerprint pointing at the source.

178
00:17:46,061 --> 00:17:53,670
Scientists now pair these maps with carbon dioxide measurements to estimate how much carbon each hot spot releases.

179
00:17:56,067 --> 00:18:00,401
OCO-2 was never designed to measure individual smokestacks.

180
00:18:00,821 --> 00:18:15,693
Yet when its narrow swath happened to pass directly over a large coal power plant, researchers could pick out the plume as a small bump in carbon dioxide, a few parts per million above the surroundings, and use it to estimate the plant's emissions.

181
00:18:16,113 --> 00:18:18,884
It was proof that power plants could be checked from orbit.

182
00:18:21,275 --> 00:18:31,656
The same team measured the dome of extra carbon dioxide that sits over Los Angeles, and found it several parts per million higher than the surrounding desert air.

183
00:18:32,076 --> 00:18:45,905
They even spotted carbon dioxide from the Yasur volcano in the South Pacific, a measurement that could one day help warn of eruptions, since volcanoes often release more carbon dioxide before they erupt.

184
00:18:48,317 --> 00:18:54,770
In May of twenty nineteen, a SpaceX Dragon delivered OCO-3 to the International Space Station.

185
00:18:55,190 --> 00:19:01,332
It was built largely from OCO-2's spare parts, but with a new trick: an agile pointing mirror.

186
00:19:01,752 --> 00:19:11,865
Instead of a single narrow strip, it can sweep back and forth and build a snapshot map of an entire city, roughly eighty by eighty kilometers, in about two minutes.

187
00:19:14,275 --> 00:19:16,657
The space station's orbit is different, too.

188
00:19:17,077 --> 00:19:26,573
It is not sun synchronous, so OCO-3 passes over the same places at different times of day: morning traffic, afternoon heat, evening rush.

189
00:19:26,993 --> 00:19:33,119
It covers the band of the planet between about fifty two degrees north and south, where most of humanity lives.

190
00:19:39,025 --> 00:19:43,012
Carbon dioxide is not the only carbon gas we are changing.

191
00:19:43,432 --> 00:19:52,316
Methane, the main ingredient of natural gas, traps about eighty times more heat than carbon dioxide over its first twenty years in the air.

192
00:19:52,736 --> 00:19:57,385
Its concentration has more than doubled since before the industrial age.

193
00:19:57,805 --> 00:20:04,796
This map is real: measurements spanning six months of twenty twenty four from the TROPOMI instrument, averaged together.

194
00:20:07,192 --> 00:20:08,864
The hot spots tell stories.

195
00:20:09,284 --> 00:20:12,488
Rice paddies and wetlands across South and East Asia.

196
00:20:12,908 --> 00:20:15,213
The vast swamps of the Sudd in South Sudan.

197
00:20:15,633 --> 00:20:17,514
But not every bright patch is real.

198
00:20:17,934 --> 00:20:28,092
Over the Sahara, very bright desert sand fools this uncorrected product into reading high, which is exactly why scientists spend years on careful corrections.

199
00:20:28,512 --> 00:20:37,168
Methane does not last as long as carbon dioxide, only about a decade, which makes cutting it one of the fastest ways to slow warming.

200
00:20:39,567 --> 00:20:43,532
Satellites have also found something more alarming: super-emitters.

201
00:20:43,952 --> 00:20:49,707
Single leaking wells, pipelines and landfills that release methane at rates of tons per hour.

202
00:20:50,127 --> 00:20:53,558
Some have been spotted from orbit, reported, and then repaired.

203
00:20:53,978 --> 00:20:58,572
Fixing a leak you can see from space is among the cheapest climate wins there is.

204
00:21:00,983 --> 00:21:05,782
Now watch carbon monoxide, a gas released when things burn incompletely.

205
00:21:06,202 --> 00:21:10,016
In late May and June of twenty twenty three, Canada was on fire.

206
00:21:10,436 --> 00:21:15,716
Each frame of this sequence is one day of real satellite measurements over North America.

207
00:21:18,108 --> 00:21:22,409
Smoke from hundreds of fires in Quebec and the West poured south and east.

208
00:21:22,829 --> 00:21:31,758
On the seventh of June, it buried New York City in an orange haze, and the air quality there briefly ranked among the worst of any major city on Earth.

209
00:21:32,178 --> 00:21:34,639
The plumes crossed the Atlantic and reached Europe.

210
00:21:37,025 --> 00:21:41,686
That season, more than fifteen million hectares of Canadian forest burned.

211
00:21:42,106 --> 00:21:52,794
Using satellite measurements of carbon monoxide, a study published in Nature estimated that the fires released around six hundred and fifty million tonnes of carbon.

212
00:21:53,214 --> 00:22:01,025
That is more than the annual fossil fuel emissions of every country on Earth except China, India and the United States.

213
00:22:03,442 --> 00:22:05,864
This is what scientists mean by a feedback.

214
00:22:06,284 --> 00:22:07,905
Warming dries out forests.

215
00:22:08,325 --> 00:22:09,598
Dry forests burn.

216
00:22:10,018 --> 00:22:11,755
Burning forests release carbon.

217
00:22:12,175 --> 00:22:13,847
More carbon means more warming.

218
00:22:14,267 --> 00:22:17,706
Satellites are how we find out how strong that loop is becoming.

219
00:22:23,608 --> 00:22:25,339
So where does all the carbon come from?

220
00:22:25,839 --> 00:22:33,936
This is humanity's yearly carbon dioxide from fossil fuels and cement, going back to seventeen fifty, split by source.

221
00:22:34,356 --> 00:22:37,213
For more than a century the line barely rises.

222
00:22:37,633 --> 00:22:40,296
Coal powered the first industrial revolution.

223
00:22:40,716 --> 00:22:41,457
Then oil.

224
00:22:41,877 --> 00:22:42,683
Then gas.

225
00:22:43,103 --> 00:22:46,376
And then, after nineteen fifty, everything accelerates.

226
00:22:48,775 --> 00:22:56,695
In twenty twenty four, fossil fuels and industry released around thirty eight and a half billion tonnes of carbon dioxide.

227
00:22:57,115 --> 00:23:02,921
Add deforestation and other land use changes, and the total is about forty three billion.

228
00:23:03,341 --> 00:23:09,478
Half of all the fossil carbon dioxide ever emitted has been released since the mid nineteen nineties.

229
00:23:11,858 --> 00:23:18,803
Break twenty twenty four down by fuel, and coal is still the biggest single source, close to sixteen billion tonnes.

230
00:23:19,223 --> 00:23:21,000
Oil adds about twelve and a half.

231
00:23:21,420 --> 00:23:22,997
Natural gas about eight.

232
00:23:23,417 --> 00:23:31,312
Cement, which releases carbon dioxide from the chemistry of making it, not just the energy, adds roughly one and a half more.

233
00:23:33,692 --> 00:23:36,978
But the atmosphere does not care who emitted carbon this year.

234
00:23:37,398 --> 00:23:38,746
It keeps a running total.

235
00:23:39,166 --> 00:23:45,084
Here is each country's cumulative emissions from fossil fuels and cement since seventeen fifty.

236
00:23:45,504 --> 00:23:49,660
Watch the order change as the industrial age spreads around the world.

237
00:23:52,067 --> 00:23:59,699
By twenty twenty four, the United States had emitted the most in total, around four hundred and thirty five billion tonnes.

238
00:24:00,119 --> 00:24:04,957
China, now by far the largest emitter each year, is second and climbing fast.

239
00:24:05,377 --> 00:24:11,261
Russia, Germany and the United Kingdom, early industrial powers, still rank near the top.

240
00:24:13,650 --> 00:24:16,193
And per person, the picture changes again.

241
00:24:16,613 --> 00:24:19,838
The average American emits about fourteen tonnes a year.

242
00:24:20,258 --> 00:24:23,366
The average person in China, around eight and a half.

243
00:24:23,786 --> 00:24:25,667
In India, about two point two.

244
00:24:26,087 --> 00:24:30,674
These differences sit at the heart of every international climate negotiation.

245
00:24:31,094 --> 00:24:38,538
And this is precisely why independent measurements from space matter: the atmosphere is the one ledger nobody can edit.

246
00:24:44,442 --> 00:24:47,939
Here is one of the strangest facts in all of climate science.

247
00:24:48,359 --> 00:24:53,451
Of all the carbon dioxide we release, only a little under half stays in the atmosphere.

248
00:24:53,871 --> 00:24:55,181
The rest disappears.

249
00:24:55,601 --> 00:25:08,169
Scientists who balance the global carbon budget each year find that the ocean absorbs roughly a quarter of our emissions, and land plants and soils absorb roughly another quarter to a third.

250
00:25:10,567 --> 00:25:18,783
The ocean takes it in by simple chemistry: carbon dioxide dissolves into cold surface water and is carried into the depths.

251
00:25:19,203 --> 00:25:20,643
The price is acidity.

252
00:25:21,063 --> 00:25:29,388
Seawater has grown measurably more acidic since the industrial age began, which makes life harder for corals, shellfish and plankton.

253
00:25:31,775 --> 00:25:33,879
The land sink is the mysterious one.

254
00:25:34,299 --> 00:25:46,596
Forests and grasslands absorb billions of tonnes of carbon a year, but how much, and exactly where, swings from year to year and has been one of the biggest uncertainties in climate science.

255
00:25:47,016 --> 00:25:48,808
Is it northern forests regrowing?

256
00:25:49,308 --> 00:25:50,213
The Amazon?

257
00:25:50,713 --> 00:25:54,287
Fertilization from the extra carbon dioxide itself?

258
00:25:56,692 --> 00:26:15,212
In twenty twenty three, scientists working with OCO-2 data published something new: estimates of carbon dioxide emissions and removals for more than one hundred countries, derived from the atmosphere itself, as a contribution to the first global stocktake under the Paris Agreement.

259
00:26:15,632 --> 00:26:17,255
Not what countries reported.

260
00:26:17,675 --> 00:26:18,882
What the air recorded.

261
00:26:19,302 --> 00:26:25,150
It is an early version of something the world has never had: an independent way to check the books.

262
00:26:27,567 --> 00:26:32,346
These natural sinks are the reason the planet has warmed only as much as it has.

263
00:26:32,766 --> 00:26:35,183
They are a gift we have been receiving for free.

264
00:26:35,603 --> 00:26:39,799
The question that hangs over the century is whether that gift will keep coming.

265
00:26:40,219 --> 00:26:48,828
That is the deepest reason missions like OCO-2 exist: not just to watch our emissions, but to watch the planet's response to them.

266
00:26:54,733 --> 00:26:56,163
Why does any of this matter?

267
00:26:56,663 --> 00:26:58,744
Because carbon dioxide traps heat.

268
00:26:59,164 --> 00:27:05,313
This is NASA's record of global surface temperature, from eighteen eighty to today, each stripe one year.

269
00:27:05,733 --> 00:27:08,658
Blue is cooler than the mid twentieth century average.

270
00:27:09,078 --> 00:27:10,042
Red is warmer.

271
00:27:12,442 --> 00:27:25,847
Twenty twenty four was the warmest year in the record, about one point three degrees Celsius above the nineteen fifty one to nineteen eighty average, and roughly one and a half degrees warmer than the late nineteenth century.

272
00:27:26,267 --> 00:27:28,203
Twenty twenty five came in second.

273
00:27:30,608 --> 00:27:38,732
In Paris, in twenty fifteen, the world's nations agreed to try to hold warming well below two degrees, and to pursue one and a half.

274
00:27:39,152 --> 00:27:41,704
Single years have now touched that lower line.

275
00:27:42,124 --> 00:27:49,716
Whether the long term average crosses it depends largely on how quickly the curve you saw at the beginning of this film stops rising.

276
00:27:52,108 --> 00:27:57,828
Plot each year's temperature against that year's carbon dioxide level, and the two climb together.

277
00:27:58,248 --> 00:27:59,698
Physics explains why.

278
00:28:00,118 --> 00:28:10,364
Carbon dioxide absorbs and re-emits infrared heat leaving the Earth's surface, something scientists first measured in the laboratory in the eighteen fifties.

279
00:28:10,784 --> 00:28:15,679
Add more of it, and the planet must warm until it sheds as much heat as it receives.

280
00:28:21,567 --> 00:28:22,633
Let us step back.

281
00:28:23,053 --> 00:28:32,268
This is one full day on the planet you live on, from midnight to midnight, as seen by GOES-19 on the September equinox of twenty twenty six.

282
00:29:06,650 --> 00:29:09,995
Every swirl of cloud is carrying carbon dioxide with it.

283
00:29:10,415 --> 00:29:14,351
The atmosphere mixes the whole planet's breath together in about a year.

284
00:29:14,771 --> 00:29:18,734
Which is why, to understand it, you have to watch it all, all the time.

285
00:29:24,650 --> 00:29:28,180
OCO-2 was not alone, and it was never meant to be.

286
00:29:28,600 --> 00:29:34,226
Japan's GOSAT pioneered greenhouse gas measurements from space in two thousand nine.

287
00:29:34,646 --> 00:29:36,037
China launched TanSat.

288
00:29:36,457 --> 00:29:41,607
Japan followed with GOSAT-2, and in twenty twenty five with GOSAT-GW.

289
00:29:42,027 --> 00:29:44,381
France launched MicroCarb that same year.

290
00:29:44,801 --> 00:29:51,578
Europe is building a constellation of carbon monitoring satellites, called CO2M, for the coming years.

291
00:29:53,983 --> 00:29:57,494
OCO-2 was designed for a two year prime mission.

292
00:29:57,914 --> 00:30:08,051
It has now been measuring the planet's breath for more than twelve years, building one of the longest and most precise records of carbon dioxide ever made from space.

293
00:30:08,471 --> 00:30:12,842
OCO-3 has been at work on the space station since twenty nineteen.

294
00:30:15,233 --> 00:30:27,312
In twenty twenty five, both missions were proposed for termination in the federal budget, even though both were working well and together cost on the order of fifteen million dollars a year to operate.

295
00:30:27,732 --> 00:30:33,782
Scientists around the world argued that switching them off would break a record that cannot be restarted.

296
00:30:36,192 --> 00:30:48,853
In August of twenty twenty six, NASA signed agreements with Caltech to operate both observatories for up to five years, with NASA retaining ownership, and operations moving to Caltech this autumn.

297
00:30:49,273 --> 00:30:51,007
For now, the watch continues.

298
00:30:53,400 --> 00:30:55,322
The next questions are already clear.

299
00:30:55,742 --> 00:30:59,790
Will the Amazon keep absorbing carbon, or tip toward releasing it?

300
00:31:00,290 --> 00:31:04,848
Will thawing Arctic permafrost release carbon frozen for thousands of years?

301
00:31:05,348 --> 00:31:07,458
Will the oceans keep taking up their share?

302
00:31:07,958 --> 00:31:10,458
None of these can be answered by models alone.

303
00:31:10,878 --> 00:31:14,450
They need measurements, continuous and global, year after year.

304
00:31:16,858 --> 00:31:18,543
Remember the counter in the corner.

305
00:31:18,963 --> 00:31:23,611
Since this film began, humanity has released this much carbon dioxide.

306
00:31:24,031 --> 00:31:28,601
Roughly half of it will linger in the air, and part of that for centuries or longer.

307
00:31:29,021 --> 00:31:35,439
The rest has already started the long journey into the oceans and the forests, for as long as they keep accepting it.

308
00:31:37,858 --> 00:31:50,783
Somewhere above you right now, a small observatory is crossing the sunlit side of the planet, splitting sunlight into a thousand colors, counting molecules in a column of air seven hundred kilometers tall.

309
00:31:51,203 --> 00:31:57,804
It sees what no one on the ground can see: a living planet, breathing in, breathing out, and slowly changing.

310
00:31:58,224 --> 00:32:02,890
The more clearly we can see that, the better our chances of choosing what happens next.
